WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
Many protocols are stateful.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:11.400
They have setup, negotiation, authentication, authorization, normal operation, shutdown, and error states.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.380
Not every message is valid in every state.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:22.000
A parser that checks only structure but ignores state can hand later code a command that is well-formed but not allowed now.

5
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.160
The state machine should be explicit where it matters.

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.780
State names, allowed transitions, and error behavior should be visible in the code.

7
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.080
This makes it easier to review whether a message is allowed before the parser dispatches it to application logic.

8
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.840
A structurally valid message may still be invalid for the current state.

9
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.740
For example, a request that is valid during normal operation may not be valid during setup.

10
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.760
A message that is valid after authorization may not be valid before authorization.

11
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.300
The parser should check both format and state before action.

12
00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:03.260
Command dispatch should happen only after framing, field validation, state checks, and authorization checks succeed.

13
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.360
Dispatch is the point where input begins to affect application behavior.

14
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.820
Reaching that point should require all relevant gates to pass.

15
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:19.660
The parser should handle unexpected sequence, repeated-looking messages, duplicates, and disconnects predictably at a defensive level.

16
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.000
Predictability is the goal.

17
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:31.060
The service should not preserve partial trust after a failed transition, and it should not let state-changing operations run from partially parsed data.
